From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 02 Oct 94 13:57:23 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-20] Genes & Memes

There is a fascinating book out now called "The History and Geography of Human
Genes" by L. Luca Cavalli-Sforza, Paolo Menozzi & Alberto Piazza.  Princeton
Univ. Press, 1994.  ISBN 0-691-08750-4.

I have previously put Cavalli-Sforza's (& Feldman's) 1981 book "Cultural
Transmission and Evolution:  A Quantitative Approach" on my recommended reading
list due to its status as the first rigorous quantitative approach to memetic
evolution (the selectional system of our higher-order consciousness as opposed
to natural selection).  This new book is simply a heroic effort, a virtual
compendium of human genetic knowledge.  Almost 1,100 8.5" x 11" pages of maps,
tables and narrative detailing the origination and distribution of the key human
allele frequencies from everywhere in the world.

This is a tour de force, blending history, ecology, climate, genes, linguistics
and physical anthropology.  Interesting observations on the close linkages over
history between linguistic memes and genes.  Upsets apple-carts, find out who is
really related to who, and how far back.

