From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 02 Oct 94 13:57:35 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-21] Penrose's "Shadows of the Mind"

  I was disappointed to see in Penrose, an inaccurate portrayal of Gerald
Edelman's theory of consciousness.  Penrose seems to think that because of
Edelman's work in developing the DARWIN series of perception machines, that
Edelman thinks the brain is a computational device.  Nothing could be farther
from the truth.  It makes me wonder if Penrose actually read anything by Edelman
or whether he felt he should put some words in his book about alternative
theories for filler.

  Edelman's reasons for developing the DARWIN series has to do with aiding his
reasoning powers in understanding the complexity of spontaneously ordered
selectional systems, not with producing consciousness.  Computation is assigned
to natural events by human observers.  Computation is not intrinsic in nature.
Nature does not compute.  Computation, along with mathematics and logic, is a
branch of linguistics, not natural science.  It's more closely related to poetry
than physics.  Hmm...  Maybe that's a bad comparison, considering where
neo-Gnostic physics has wandered off to.  Drivel about the "Omega Point" is
basically mystical poetry, without the truth-bearing characteristics of the
highest poetical genius.

