I'm familiar with Jaynes' thesis, though I admit I haven't read the book.  As
far as I recall, he has analyzed ancient literature and concluded that before
the time of Homer's Illiad human motivation has always been described in terms
of divine intervention.  He also notes the physiology of a region of the brain
(in the left temporal lobe, opposite of what in the right is Wernicke's area -
an area involved in the comprehension of speech) that when stimulated in an
awake patient leads to visions and voices of a somewhat mystical nature.  He
suggests from this that until recently, human behavior has been guided by
the subconscious mind through subtle motivational hints.  Around the time of
the Greeks, Jaynes proposes, humans have broken away from their subconscious
motivations and began thinking rationally.  Please correct if I have
misrepresented Jaynes' position.  I'm sure I've left a lot out.

While I found this theory interesting a few years ago, I'm quite skeptical
of it now.  It leads one to the conclusion that ancient pre-Greek civilizations
were built by intellects in some qualitatively way different from those
that built Rome.  I find this extremely difficult to believe, even with the
greatest amount of wishful thinking...  Another trendy theory added to the pile
of hopeful candidates (excuse the blase rejection, but as someone who is
devoting his life to the study of mind, I place my bets on more solid
methodology - still searching...).

>From: Superjason <prophet@ksu.ksu.edu>
