From: levy@cs.utk.edu
Date: Sun, 2 Oct 1994 19:51:40 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-26] LANG: Reclaiming 'slut'

This talk about "reclaiming" words like "slut" strikes me as well-motivated but
a bit naive.  The reason that a language has a word for something is that people
feel a need to express that thing.  So "reclaiming" a word will most likely 
result in either (1) people's finding another word for that thing, or (2) 
the word's being used affectionately by an in-group and pejoratively by
everyone else ("nigger", "queer", et al.).  The word "slut" has negative
connotations because female promiscuity has negative connotations. I
don't see how word usage is going to change that.

Can someone explain why I'm wrong?  (Without reference to Sapir/Whorf, which
I've never found convincing, and which focused on grammar as opposed to word
usage,anyway).

-- Simon

