From: John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us>
Date: Sun, 2 Oct 1994 22:54:58 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-29] Non Destructive Uploads ?

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

	>tsf@netcom.com (Tim Freeman) writes in X-Message-Number:#94-10-11: 
	>Does anyone have a plausible scenario for an early upload 
	>attempt that  allows more than one try?
	
If we had an X ray laser you could take a hologram of a brain
and that would contain an enormous amount of information. We
might not be able to extract all that information now ( it would
take a lot of computational power) but it would be there for
future use. Up to now the only way to make a X ray laser was
with a nuclear bomb, but last month a great deal of progress was
made to make it practical. Dr. Charles K. Rhodes  at the
University of Illinois was able to confirm theoretical
predictions that  clusters of Xenon atoms can admit hard X rays
( 2 angstroms) just by exciting them with UV light. I don't know
how intense the X rays would have to be for a detailed hologram so
I don't know if this would be non destructive. I know MRI would
be harmless.

 A run of the mill Magnetic Resonance Imaging machine (MRI) can
make a 3D image of an entire LIVING , conscious brain without
radiation. It can revel structures so small it would take a good
magnifying glass to see. If the resolution were 100 times better
perhaps we would have enough information to upload , especially
if we also had on record the persons genetic code and so knew
the general layout of that particular brain.

I can think of two ways to increase resolution , there are probably 
many others.

  1) Improve the software. Recently Dr. Aaron G Filler of the
University of Washington was able to image tiny nerve fibers for
the first time. He did this by rewriting the software that 
interprets the faint signals given off by a MRI machine and
convert them into pictures.  Dr. Filler , with the help of Dr.
Franklyn Howe of the University of London has used it on a
patient to get a good image of the large sciatic nerve. This has
never been done before with a MRI. His software blocked out
signals from fat and other unwanted tissue  around the nerve. It
even blocked out unwanted signals from the sheathing tissue
within the nerve so individual nerve bundles (fascicles) could
be clearly seen. This was done by a standard MRI  machine but
with new software.

  2) Increase the magnetic field. The average hospital MRI has a
field of about 3000 gauss although  some cheap models only have
500. The strongest magnetic field ever produced was 372,000
gauss  at the National Magnet Laboratory at MIT. Using a field
this strong in a MRI machine big enough to scan a brain would 
not be a trivial endeavor but is probably easier than
nanotechnology. As far as I know  no new science  would be
needed just excellent engineering.

								
